// Quiz: Regions and Availability Zones

::Q1::What is an AWS region?
{
    ~A single data center containing multiple availability zones
    =A separate geographic area containing its own AWS infrastructure
    ~A small site used primarily to cache content near end users
    ~A logical grouping of resources that can span all AWS locations
}

::Q2::A company serves customers primarily in Europe, and regulations require its personal data to remain within a specific European jurisdiction. Which factor should primarily determine the AWS region it chooses?
{
    =Compliance and data residency
    ~Cost
    ~Latency
    ~Number of edge locations
}

::Q3::Why are AWS availability zones physically separated within a region?
{
    ~To make every zone operate as a separate AWS region
    ~To ensure that resources in different zones have different pricing
    ~To cache frequently requested content closer to end users
    =To reduce the chance that a localized failure affects multiple zones
}

::Q4::What is the primary purpose of an AWS edge location?
{
    ~To run the primary application infrastructure for a region
    =To cache content close to end users for faster delivery
    ~To provide an independent set of availability zones for a region
    ~To store every resource created in an AWS account
}

::Q5::An AWS resource was created successfully, but it does not appear when the user runs a command. What should the user check first?
{
    ~The number of availability zones in the region
    ~The nearest edge location
    =The configured AWS region
    ~The physical data center where the resource is stored
}
